<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=genetically-modified-crops">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Genetically modified crops</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1983">1983</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as GM crops</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Genetically modified crops are plants used in agriculture whose DNA has been changed using genetic engineering methods. The first genetically engineered crop plant was tobacco, reported in 1983, made by joining an antibiotic resistance gene to a plasmid from the bacterium Agrobacterium. The first field trials of such plants took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the US in 1986, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was the first country to commercialise transgenic plants, with a virus-resistant tobacco in 1992. The Flavr Savr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomato</a> was approved for sale in the US in 1994. Acreage grew from 1.7 million hectares in 1996 to 185.1 million hectares in 2016.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since the 1970s growing public awareness and advances in environmental science, ecology and technology have brought new concerns to the fore: ozone depletion, climate change, acid rain and <b>genetically modified crops</b> and livestock.</blockquote><a href="/movement/environmental-movement">Environmental movement · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
